From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix: sanitize GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE atom

The arm64 Steam client sends a broken night-mode vector: the hue field only
sweeps cold->red (~0.5 up to 1.0, HW-measured) and never a warm value, the
saturation field is frozen garbage (~6.5e22), and only `amount` is usable (it
tracks the intensity slider). gamescope renders HSV(hue, saturation*amount, 1.0)
faithfully, so those values turn night mode into a magenta/red screen instead of
the expected warm amber.

This was first read as a consequence of this panel exposing no EDID, but other
arm64 systems on unrelated panels hit the identical vector, so the cause is the
client and not our display stack. Worth stating because it rules out the
"advertise real colorimetry via a kernel panel EDID" route a second time: even a
perfect EDID would not change what the client writes into this atom.

Clamp amount/saturation into [0,1] and force hue to amber unless it lies in a
tight warm band (excluding both hue extremes, since 0.0 and 1.0 are pure red),
right where the atom is decoded. Night mode becomes a reliable warm cast whose
strength follows the intensity slider, without a kernel-provided panel EDID.
---
 src/steamcompmgr.cpp | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6349,6 +6349,28 @@
 		nightmode.hue = vec[1];
 		nightmode.saturation = vec[2];
 
+		// novadeck: sanitize the night-mode vector before it drives the color pipeline.
+		// On our no-EDID DSI panel the Steam client sends a broken tint: the hue field only
+		// ever sweeps cold->red (~0.5 up to 1.0, HW-measured) and never a warm value, and the
+		// saturation field is frozen garbage (~6.5e22); only `amount` is usable (it tracks the
+		// intensity slider). gamescope renders HSV(hue, sat*amount, 1) faithfully, so those
+		// bogus values turn night mode magenta/red. Clamp amount/saturation into [0,1] and
+		// force hue to amber unless it lies in a tight warm band, so night mode is a reliable
+		// warm cast whose strength follows the intensity slider. The band is deliberately
+		// narrow and excludes both hue extremes (0.0 and 1.0 are pure red) — a value near 1.0
+		// is the client's garbage, NOT a "deep red" the user asked for. See TODO.md
+		// "Display color controls" + steam-gamescope-handshake HW notes.
+		{
+			constexpr float kNightModeAmberHue = 25.0f / 360.0f;  // 25deg amber, matches Steam Deck night mode
+			constexpr float kNightModeWarmHueMin = 0.02f; // exclude pure red (harsh) at hue 0
+			constexpr float kNightModeWarmHueMax = 0.20f; // amber .. yellow upper bound
+			const bool bWarmHue = nightmode.hue >= kNightModeWarmHueMin && nightmode.hue <= kNightModeWarmHueMax;
+			if ( !std::isfinite( nightmode.hue ) || !bWarmHue )
+				nightmode.hue = kNightModeAmberHue;
+			nightmode.saturation = std::isfinite( nightmode.saturation ) ? std::clamp( nightmode.saturation, 0.0f, 1.0f ) : 1.0f;
+			nightmode.amount = std::isfinite( nightmode.amount ) ? std::clamp( nightmode.amount, 0.0f, 1.0f ) : 0.0f;
+		}
+
 		if ( set_color_nightmode( nightmode ) )
 			hasRepaint = true;
 	}
